WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:08.670
Ask a bunch of main framers what makes their platform so powerful, and there's a good chance

2
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.470
a good number of them will tell you it's Sysplex.

3
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:17.710
Simply put, Sysplex is a way of making multiple systems work together as a team, and this

4
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:23.030
can be L-Pars on one physical box, or it can be spread out across many.

5
00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:27.110
Before we get into exactly how it does this, we first have to define a couple of basic

6
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.110
concepts.

7
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:32.270
First, there's STP, the server-time protocol.

8
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.350
Systems need to be an exact agreement about time, like exact agreement.

9
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.430
And that kind of makes sense if you've got multiple systems writing to the same file,

10
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.150
milliseconds apart, and you're trying to figure out which one got to that piece of

11
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.550
data first.

12
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.190
Having your watch is synchronized is kind of important.

13
00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.870
Then there's GRS, the global resource serialization.

14
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.670
This allows for multiple systems to access the same resources concurrently, serializing

15
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.110
where necessary to ensure exclusive access.

16
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.310
Without GRS, you have quite a mess.

17
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.310
That's a handy rhyme.

18
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:11.110
You should remember that, in case you're tested on it later.

19
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.550
XCF, the cross coupling facility.

20
00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:17.590
This manages communication between applications and Sysplex.

21
00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.630
This also lets you be logged into one system, and issue commands on behalf of another

22
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.070
system in that Sysplex.

23
00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:28.830
This right here is what lets the Sysplex communicate as a whole.

24
00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.870
Next up, the coupling links.

25
00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.190
These connect L-Pars to processors.

26
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.390
Without these accessing memory on different physical systems, would have to run through

27
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.270
one of those L-Pars.

28
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.510
And these allow direct memory access communications between Sysplex memory and the memory

29
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.030
of an attached system.

30
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.550
Finally, there's the CDS, the couple data set.

31
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.150
So imagine you've got a bunch of people working on the same job.

32
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.830
Let's just say they're tallying up the numbers of different colored candies they're

33
00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.630
getting from various bags of candies.

34
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.630
Bags of candies.

35
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.630
Anyway, they're all working together to get one final result, which means they're all working

36
00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.630
on the same set of data.

37
00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:13.310
Now in order to coordinate this operation, so someone doesn't accidentally overwrite someone

38
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.710
else's work, there needs to be an additional file that keeps track of who is currently

39
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.230
working on a resource.

40
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.870
So everyone knows how to wait their turn.

41
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.430
That's the CDS.

42
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.990
These parts are super important when you're talking about Sysplex, because without them,

43
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.430
you've just got another distributed environment.

44
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.470
Those memory links, the tightly coupled resources, and of course being an absolute lock

45
00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.750
step on time is the only way this will all work.

46
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.830
We talked a little bit earlier about WLM, the workload manager.

47
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.990
It's the component that makes sure that high priority work gets run quickly and that

48
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.990
all of our targets are met.

49
00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.950
WLM makes quite a bit of use of Sysplex to help balance things out.

50
00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.750
So if that discussion earlier about performance tuning on the mainframe was interesting

51
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.390
to you, Sysplex is something you want to pay close attention to.

52
00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:07.230
Sysplex is, at its core, a bunch of ZOS operating systems working together.

53
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:12.750
They're able to cooperatively use hardware, software, and components to achieve a high availability

54
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.270
workload processing environment.

55
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.830
This gets us back to the whole physical logical discussion, and when you have systems

56
00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.830
in a Sysplex, when you're talking about how they're going to handle the work, you essentially

57
00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.390
have one entity that is running the work.

58
00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.070
It's the Sysplex.

59
00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:32.630
Even though you know that you've got multiple physical mainframes and within those multiple

60
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:38.470
logical L-Pars, you'll often hear people just refer to it all collectively as the Sysplex

61
00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.990
or just the Plex.

62
00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.710
Let's talk about those Sysplex configurations.

63
00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.870
There's three main ways you can configure a Sysplex.

64
00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.430
Now, bit of a riddle.

65
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.670
If you've got a Sysplex with only one L-Par in it, is it really a Sysplex?

66
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.350
The answer is yes, and it's called a monoplex.

67
00:03:56.350 --> 00:04:00.630
Now, you'll often see a monoplex in testing and staging situations.

68
00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:06.030
But beyond that, it doesn't really buy you any additional performance or functionality.

69
00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.790
Then there's this concept of a base Sysplex.

70
00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.030
In this configuration, every system has a connection to every other system.

71
00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:16.870
And they all know about each other and they all agree to do work together.

72
00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.750
It's almost like they started a little band.

73
00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:23.630
The most common form of a Sysplex, though, is the parallel Sysplex.

74
00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:28.310
In this configuration, instead of needing every system connected to every other system,

75
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.030
we have this concept of a coupling facility.

76
00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.830
This is a special type of L-Par that you bring up.

77
00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:37.790
And typically, you have backups of it on other mainframes, too.

78
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.750
In a parallel Sysplex, the coupling facility handles all the complexities and

79
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.910
overhead of those systems working together.

80
00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:51.110
So you can have up to 32 ZOS images and up to 32 physical mainframes.

81
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:57.310
That would mean one L-Par on each mainframe hooked into a 32-way Sysplex.

82
00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.710
Now, most systems aren't scaled out that way.

83
00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.590
But if you need to, you could do it.

84
00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.950
Now, a word about availability.

85
00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:09.190
You might hear people say that a mainframe environment hasn't gone down in decades.

86
00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:13.670
But how is that possible, and that's the running on 20-year-old hardware and software?

87
00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:17.910
Well, one of the neat things about being an Sysplex is you can do what's called a rolling

88
00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.150
IPL.

89
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:24.030
An IPL is just booting up an operating system, and a parallel Sysplex doesn't require

90
00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:29.150
all the systems in it to be up in order to function, just enough to keep it running.

91
00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:35.550
So if I've got 16 L-Pars in my Sysplex, we're just saying, I can bring down four of them,

92
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.510
help date the operating systems on them, and then bring them back up and put them into

93
00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:40.990
the plex.

94
00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:44.150
L-Pars got upgraded, and the plex stayed up.

95
00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.870
I can even do this with physical systems as long as some of the members are running elsewhere

96
00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.390
in another mainframe.

97
00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:53.230
So let's go back to those 16 L-Pars.

98
00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.070
Say they're spread out across four mainframes, just as an example.

99
00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.870
And we want to upgrade one of those mainframes to a shiny brand new one.

100
00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:05.750
We bring down the four L-Pars on that system that we want to replace, physically swap

101
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:10.390
it out with a new one, cable it up, configure it the way we want to, power it back on,

102
00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:16.310
IPL, or boot up those four systems on the new hardware, very them back into the plex,

103
00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.590
and we're good to go.

104
00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.110
The plex never went down.

105
00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.070
That is some pretty amazing stuff, and you can see why so many people would tell you it's

106
00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.710
their favorite feature.

107
00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.710
Although it's hard to pick just one, isn't it?
